{"id":"h118","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Assume all gases are perfect unless stated otherwise. Note that 1 atm = 1.013 25 bar. Unless otherwise stated, thermochemical data are for 298.15 K. An average human produces about $10 \\mathrm{MJ}$ of heat each day through metabolic activity. Human bodies are actually open systems, and the main mechanism of heat loss is through the evaporation of water. What mass of water should be evaporated each day to maintain constant temperature?\nIs the correct answer \"8.5 kg\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct mass of water is about 4.1 kg, not 8.5 kg.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Nothing in the problem clearly supports 8.5 kg as the answer.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"8.5 kg would require an energy cost of about 1.2 MJ per kg, far below water's latent heat of vaporization.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Evaporating water absorbs heat equal to its enthalpy of vaporization.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"At 298.15 K the enthalpy of vaporization of water is about 44.0 kJ/mol, or about 2.44 MJ/kg.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Even the 100 °C latent heat (about 2.26 MJ/kg) gives roughly 4.4 kg, not 8.5 kg.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Evaporation at body temperature is the relevant process, so the 298 K value applies.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Dividing 10 MJ by 2.44 MJ/kg gives about 4.1 kg, or about 230 mol of water.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"8.5 kg would need an energy cost roughly half the actual value.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-1,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"rela